# Woodright release & runtime governance

Complements `woodright-core.mdc`, `woodright-response-format.mdc`, `foreground-only-execution.mdc`, `review-server-stability-gate.mdc`, `github-access.mdc`, `personal-data-mac-safety.mdc`.

## Status taxonomy (no bare `done`)

Use exactly one:

| Status | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `audited_only` | Audit only; no product change |
| `implemented_locally` | Code changed; runtime not verified |
| `candidate_verified` | Candidate API + hydrated DOM OK; canonical unchanged |
| `integrated_not_deployed` | In `main`; runtime not updated |
| `deployed_not_verified` | Runtime updated; public DOM gate incomplete |
| `done_deployed_and_verified` | Public hydrated DOM + digests + integrity + ≥5 samples |
| `done_governance_merged_no_deploy` | Governance/CI merged; active runtime intentionally unchanged |
| `done_governance_merged_no_deploy_with_known_dokploy_bypass` | Governance merged; runtime unchanged; Dokploy UI residual bypass explicitly declared (not closed) |
| `rolled_back` | New release rolled back; previous restored |
| `blocked` | Cannot continue safely |
| `unsafe` | Must not leave runtime/changes as-is |

`done_deployed_and_verified` **requires**: public URL, exact digests, public DOM evidence, rollback identity, five stable samples. API-only or candidate-only is never enough.

## Immutable digest only

Cutover / restart pin is `image@sha256:…` only. Mutable tags (including SHA-like tags) may help locate an image but are **not** release identity. Tag drift after build → document drift; **do not** redeploy from the drifted tag.

## Backend / storefront SHA parity

One release = one exact Git SHA for both images. Exception: documented emergency rollback to a previously verified digest pair.

## Candidate ≠ canonical ≠ public

Always name separately: candidate URL/ports, canonical local (`:3002`→`:9000`), public URL, each storefront’s backend upstream, each SHA/digest.

## API ≠ UI

Correct JSON order is supporting evidence only. Buyer-facing tasks need hydrated DOM after SSR, hydration, filters, grouping, navigation, sort reset.

## Single runtime owner

One controller per runtime pair. Forbidden simultaneous control via Dokploy + Compose + systemd + cron + Nightly + LaunchAgent + ad-hoc shell. Pre-cutover owner audit required. `ACTIVE_OWNER.json` and `ACTIVE-RUNTIME-OWNER.txt` must agree or deploy is blocked.

## Rollback before cutover

Capture previous digests, keepers, commands, backup dir, health baseline **before** live swap. Do not invent rollback after failure.

## Five public samples

After cutover: ≥5 public samples (timestamp, status, marker, first item, critical DOM, digest/SHA if available). Divergence → competing owner / stale replica / cache split → not done.

## Clean release source

Build from a clean worktree at an exact commit. No dirty WIP, no foreign `.next`, no uncommitted release source.

## No blind cherry-pick

Three-way semantic audit (old parent, source commit, current main) before integrate. Text merge ≠ semantic OK.

## Data integrity + no unauthorized mutations

Before/after catalog releases: count, IDs, visibility, prices, images, collections, critical PDP, cart smoke. Do not mutate catalog/prices/SKU/media/schema/motifs without a separate explicit scope.

## Markers support digests

Markers (e.g. `x-woodright-catalog-order`) support identity; they do not replace digests, Git provenance, DOM, health, or integrity.

## Evidence outside Git

Screenshots, logs, PID, traces, inspect JSON → `/tmp` or task dir. Never commit runtime evidence into product commits.

## Deployment report identity

Every deploy packet must list: release SHA, backend/storefront digests, container IDs, workflow run, owner, previous digests, rollback path, public verification URL.

## Owner worktrees

Never clean/reset/checkout/delete the owner’s dirty worktree without separate explicit permission. Integrate in a new clean worktree.


## Build ≠ release (21)

Successful image build is not a release. Build workflows must not change active owner/release manifests, rollback keepers, or claim deployed. Authorization = separate release manifest + cutover.

## Unique build execution identity (22–24)

Every build needs source SHA + workflow run ID + run attempt. Unique discovery tag: `build-<sha>-run-<runId>-attempt-<attempt>`. Re-attempts get a new tag. SHA-like tags remain **mutable** registry pointers; digest is the only immutable identity.

## Build arguments in provenance (25)

Non-secret build arg **names** and a fingerprint belong in the build manifest. Never store secret values.

## Active release manifest required (26–27)

Before the next cutover, server must have a validated active release manifest (+ checksum). Owner TXT/JSON alone are insufficient. Owner + release state updates are transactional (temp → validate → atomic rename).

## Evidence durability (28, 40)

`/tmp` is not an owner-review archive. Important artifacts need regenerable scripts, durable copy outside repo or CI retention, and SHA-256 checksums.

## Candidate lifecycle (29–30)

Every candidate needs owner/task/ports/SHA/review_after/public_route flags. Expiry means review, not auto-delete.

## In-progress workflows (31)

Relevant image builds must be resolved or explicitly marked unresolved in the handoff; do not use their tags until provenance completes.

## Data fallback measurable (32–34)

Catalog audits must count missing category/collection, title fallback, unknown tier, ambiguous classification. Owner-review CSV/JSON must be regenerable from a timestamped API snapshot. No silent fallback expansion.

## Governance merge ≠ deploy (35)

Rules/docs/CI merges must not change active runtime. Image build after merge ≠ deploy. Unexpected cutover = governance violation.

## Tag namespace ownership (36)

Each image-writing workflow declares `woodright.tag.namespace`. No silent shared authoritative tags.

## Partial pair not releasable (37)

Backend without matching storefront (same SHA, both digests, tests) is not a release.

## Restart reuses active digests (38–39)

Restart uses digests from the active release manifest. Do not re-resolve mutable tags. Registry tag movement ≠ live runtime change.


## Global cutover lock (41)

Any live cutover, restart, rollback, or container replacement must use one canonical lock: `/srv/woodright/locks/live-cutover.lock` (flock). Competing per-path locks without this lock are forbidden.

## Compare-and-swap (42–43, 55)

Live mutation requires `expected_current_release_sha` + expected backend/storefront digests matching ACTIVE_RELEASE, ACTIVE_OWNER, and live containers immediately before mutation. External change → abort `aborted_conflict`; do not chase live blindly.

## Reconciliation ≠ deploy (44–45)

ACTIVE_RELEASE must not be updated from container existence alone. Provenance + health + public verification required. Post-external metadata write is `reconciled_external_cutover`, never claimed as this agent’s deploy.

## Cutover transaction (46–49)

Every cutover/rollback has a transaction ID and validated state machine (`planned`…`active`/`rolled_back`/`failed`/`aborted_conflict`). ACTIVE_RELEASE is written only after public buyer-visible gate. Rollback uses the same lock, CAS, and verification.

## Restart / identity (50, 52–53)

Restart reuses exact digests from ACTIVE_RELEASE (no mutable tag resolve). Owner/manifest/Config.Image/RepoDigests/OCI revisions must agree. Unknown live image without Git/workflow/digest provenance is critical.

## Audit + drift (51, 54)

Cutover audit log is append-only with hash chain. Long governance tasks re-check live identity before any metadata write.

## Candidate / DQ / aborted dirs (56–59)

Review date ≠ cleanup permission. Live DQ is GET-only and names source release + checksums. Empty aborted release directories need reference audit before removal.

## Operator identity (60)

Cutover/reconciliation events record actor/controller without credentials.

## Release bundle may contain multiple source revisions (61)

If backend and storefront have different revisions, the manifest must describe both. Never present the storefront SHA as the SHA of the whole pair.

## Bundle ID is not a Git SHA (62)

A split release receives a separate immutable `bundle_id` (e.g. `wrb-…-be…-sf…`). Bundle identity must not be a bare Git SHA.

## Component provenance is mandatory (63)

Each component has its own revision, workflow run, attempt, unique build tag, digest, OCI labels, and PR reference.

## Split pair requires compatibility evidence (64)

SF-only or BE-only cutover is allowed only with a compatibility contract and buyer-visible QA. HTTP 200 alone is insufficient.

## ACTIVE_RELEASE points to a bundle (65)

ACTIVE_RELEASE must reference a bundle manifest (path + checksum), not only a single Git SHA.

## Primary revision must not hide component revision (66)

Optional primary/revision fields never replace backend and storefront component revisions.

## Dokploy bypass must be closed or declared (67)

Do not claim global lock coverage while Dokploy can mutate live directly. Residual UI bypass must be explicit in machine-readable policy (`claim_bypass_closed=false`).

## Decorative wrappers do not count (68)

A wrapper is not enforcement if a direct Dokploy path remains available without the gate.

## Sole-owner policy must be machine-readable (69)

If Dokploy is sole mutation owner, ACTIVE_OWNER (and validators) must record sole owner and forbid manual mutation paths.

## Mutation leases are single-use (70)

A lease cannot be reused or used after expiry. Lease does not replace CAS or the transaction log.

## Host tooling must be reproducible (71)

Missing host Node/Python must not make governance gates fictional. Use a pinned tools runner.

## Server tool images must be digest-pinned (72)

Governance tools run only from an exact `image@sha256:…`. Mutable tags are rejected.

## DTO absence is not data absence (73)

Absence of a field in a Store DTO does not prove absence in source data.

## DQ audits must name field sources (74)

Every DQ metric names the endpoint/source used. Field-source matrix is required for authoritative packets.

## Projection gaps and data gaps are separate (75)

Owner-review packets must not mix backend data remediation with API projection remediation.

## Title fallback is secondary evidence (76)

Title inference applies only after structured-source checks.

## Live DQ uses exact bundle identity (77)

DQ packets record `bundle_id`, backend revision, storefront revision, and digests.

## Split rollback is component-aware (78)

Rollback manifests distinguish pair rollback, storefront-only rollback, and backend-only rollback (only if the contract allows).

## Component cutover must update bundle metadata (79)

After SF-only/BE-only cutover, active state becomes a new bundle - not the old release with a silently swapped container.

## Metadata migration must not change runtime (80)

Manifest v1 → v2 migration is not a restart or deploy. Before/after container IDs and digests must be proven unchanged.

## Control-plane authority must be technical (81)

Documentation is not enforcement if a direct mutation path remains technically available.

## Dokploy UI bypass must be tested (82)

`claim_bypass_closed=true` is allowed only after a safe negative test of the direct UI path.

## Break-glass access must survive enforcement (83)

Never close a UI bypass by losing the only admin or recovery path.

## Auto-deploy is disabled unless explicitly authorized (84)

Build or Git merge must not automatically change live runtime without a separate release transaction.

## Every runtime stack is registered (85)

Every Woodright stack has machine-readable owner, role, revisions, ports, and cleanup policy.

## Container name is not environment identity (86)

Environment is defined by routes, owner state, bundle, and registry - not by container name.

## Public route must be unique (87)

One public domain must not point at multiple non-identical stacks.

## Non-public stacks require explicit role (88)

A stack without a public route still needs owner/task/role.

## Review date is not cleanup approval (89)

A review date starts an audit; it does not authorize stop/delete.

## Category and collection decisions are independent (90)

Do not derive collection from category or category from collection without a separate rule and evidence.

## Null collection may be intentional (91)

`collection=null` is not an error until business expectation says otherwise.

## Owner data decisions are explicit (92)

An agent proposal is not owner approval.

## High confidence does not mean auto-apply (93)

High-confidence rows still require an authorized decision workflow.

## Data mutations use exact product IDs (94)

Never apply catalog mutation by title, position, or fuzzy matching.

## Every mutation has before and after (95)

A mutation manifest stores exact before/after and a source fingerprint.

## Data mutations are reversible (96)

Every changed field has a rollback value.

## Dry-run is mandatory (97)

No catalog mutation without a reproducible dry-run.

## Code release and data mutation are separate transactions (98)

A code deploy must not silently apply catalog data changes.

## Owner-review packets have checksums (99)

A decision packet is bound to an exact source bundle and checksum.

## DTO gaps never enter owner decision queue (100)

Engineering projection gaps must not force the owner to reclassify products.

## Active state and intent are different entities (101)

Planned release intent must not live inside active owner state.

## Owner TXT is a view, not source of truth (102)

The human-readable owner file is generated from machine-readable active state and the intent registry.

## ACTIVE_RELEASE must match live containers (103)

A stale active pointer is an integrity incident and requires reconciliation.

## ACTIVE_OWNER cannot override bundle provenance (104)

Owner metadata without component provenance is insufficient for active reconciliation.

## External security cutover is reconciled, not reclaimed (105)

A governance agent must not attribute an external cutover to itself.

## Every release intent has a final status (106)

An intent must not remain indefinitely as free text in an owner file.

## Superseded intents are preserved (107)

Superseded/abandoned intents keep evidence and are not silently deleted.

## Active view must not contain stale intent SHA (108)

Owner TXT must not present a pending SHA as the active identity.

## Component digest forensics precede reconciliation (109)

ACTIVE_RELEASE must not be updated without proven OCI revisions/workflow runs.

## Metadata divergence is an incident (110)

Container vs ACTIVE_RELEASE divergence is recorded in the audit log with duration and cause.

## Container recreate requires attribution (111)

Any unexpected recreate of a non-public stack must receive a controller/actor verdict.

## Unattributed mutation is not normal maintenance (112)

An unknown mutation remains a risk; it is not dismissed as “someone updated it”.

## Rollback keepers are not live (113)

Exited keeper IDs must not remain in ACTIVE_RELEASE.

## Routing defines live role (114)

Live is defined by active route + containers + bundle, not a historical owner file.

## Baseline text is not product identity (115)

A displayed title change requires ID/handle/group checks before calling a regression.

## Group representative changes are explicit (116)

If grouping selects another representative, QA evidence must record it.

## Owner decision cells default to unapproved (117)

An empty field or agent recommendation is not approval.

## Collection null proposals name intent (118)

Null collection is classified as intentionally unassigned, missing, unknown, or legacy.

## Engineering gaps stay out of owner approval (119)

DTO/projection gaps do not enter the owner mutation manifest.

## Reconciliation must prove no runtime change (120)

Before/after container IDs, digests, and routes are mandatory for metadata-only reconciliation.
